{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>When the cloud lingered, etc.</b> Meaning, the verse recounts here the praise of Israel: that even when the cloud tarried an entire year, they would camp willingly; <b>and when it was lifted</b>, even if it tarried no more than from evening to morning and the cloud rose — they would journey. And the verse equates the two: the desire for journeying that they had when it tarried only one night is equal to the desire they had to journey after a full year, for assuredly they would yearn to travel; and the verse equates the desire they had to journey after a year to the desire to journey after a single night of encampment — for they were not short-tempered (קצים) with the delay of encampment on account of the duration not being as long as the encampment of a full year. And the verse concludes the reason: <b>at the mouth of HaShem</b> — they desired nothing but to fulfill the word of HaShem. And its saying they observed <b>et the charge of HaShem</b> means: they would wait precisely the measure of time that HaShem would wait to journey, whether little or much." "<b>And if</b> you say: if so, why was it necessary to precede with two earlier verses — the verse <b>and when the cloud lingered</b> and the verse <b>and there were times when</b>, etc. — if it is to declare Israel's praise, behold it is an even greater praise in the conclusion of the matter as we have explained. For if the statement <b>and when the cloud lingered over the mishkan</b>, etc., and <b>and there were</b>, etc., had not preceded, we would not have interpreted the verse ״and when it lingered over the mishkan, etc., at the mouth of HaShem״, etc., in the manner I explained — rather, we would have said simply that they waited during the long period and journeyed during the short period and did not rush ahead or lag behind; but the knowledge of their complete desire, in the manner I explained, we would not have known. Only after it preceded that they did not rush ahead or lag behind in the earlier verses — in the manner we explained, that not even in thought (כחושב) did they contemplate rushing ahead or lagging — did the verse come and alert us, in the conclusion of the matter, to understand what we explained concerning them. And what it further says, <b>at the mouth of HaShem by the hand of Moses</b> — our Sages of blessed memory already expounded (Sifrei Zuta, ch. 4) that the cloud would roll up and would not stand erect until Moses would say ״קוּמָה HaShem״, etc."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:07:17.969791659Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}